\begin_layout Section
Mega2 Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During an association or linkage analysis project, one may need to analyze
 the data with several different programs.
 Unfortunately, it can often be quite difficult to get one's data in the
 proper format desired by each different computer program.
 Not only must the data be converted to the proper format, but also the
 loci must be reordered into their proper order.
 Writing custom reformatting scripts can be error-prone and very time-consuming.
 To address these problems, we created Mega2
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega2,_the_Manipulation_Environment_for_Genetic_Anal
ysis
\end_layout

\end_inset

, which can be obtained from 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/register/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or via BitBucket 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://bitbucket.org/dweeks/mega2
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The early Mega2 could read input data in only a few formats: LINKAGE format
 and Mega2 format.
 The Mega2 format allowed you to specify additional information in a more
 straightfoward way than can be done with LINKAGE format.
 For example, trait locus penetrance information can be specified as well
 as the allele frequencies (Of course, the frequencies are calculated if
 not provided in a file.) The earliest versions of Mega2 were written in
 the C computer language.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Over time, Mega2 was upgraded to the C++ computer language and some of the
 internals were rewritten.
 Support for reformatting for more linkage analysis programs was added to
 Mega2.
 The data formats for genetic data changed over time; and Mega2 was enhanced
 to read input data in PLINK format, VCF format and IMPUTE2 format.
 The volume of genome-wide marker data increased significantly, so Mega2
 was extended to support the compression of biallelic genotype data (though
 still supporting microsatellite and other polymorphic data as non-compressed
 legacy data).
 As the volume of genotype data increased, it became slow and tedious to
 validate the genotype data and generate allele frequency data for each
 separate analysis that Mega2 was used for.
 Rather, Mega2 was restructured so that the validation and meta data generation
 were performed once for a given data set and stored in an SQLite3
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://www.sqlite.org/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 database file.
 Mega2 uses this database, via a menu-driven interface, to provide the data
 needed for any particular analysis.
 The same database provides the data for all the different analyses.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Sqlite3 Database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Initially, Mega2 used C (language) structures to store its intermediate
 data before analysis.
 Several alternative strategies were considered to dump these data for subsequen
t reuse without having to recompute, reanalyze, and revalidate the data.
 The first choice was to dump the raw structure data to disk.
 Re-read the data is fast but it is much harder to inspect the data for
 debugging.
 Also, the internal pointers would have to be relocated to new storage areas.
 A better solution, for many reasons, was to create a SQLite table for each
 C structure and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

insert
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the C structure data into the table.
 The data are then inherently inspectable and there are database tools to
 help to selectively extract data.
 The data are portable and can be shared on different platforms.
 Since interfaces from many languages are provided for SQLite, the data
 are accessible in many languages besides C.
 Of particular interest is R because there is a wealth of existing genetic
 analysis programs already created, well documented, and available at both
 CRAN and Bioconductor.
 Also, researchers regularly use R to develop new analysis algorithms because
 R has been used in the past to develop statistical programs and has many
 useful libraries .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the new design, there are SQL tables for loci data, marker data, allele
 data, map data, phenotype data and genotype data as well as pedigree and
 person data.
 For completness, Appendix A shows all the tables and their fields.
 (The example section, coming next, also lists the tables, in passing.) Here,
 we will make a few general observations about the tables.
 The first issue was mentioned previously: What to do with pointers.
 Where necessary, a field was added to a table, we call a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

link
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 field, viz.
 locus_link.
 This field is a unique monotonic integer.
 When the data are read back in, these links are looked up to find a pointer
 to use.
 Consider the locus_table and the allele_table.
 They both have a locus_link.
 The link in the locus_table is hashed to yield a pointer to the corresponding
 entry in the locus table.
 When the allele_table is read, the locus_link is looked up, then a pointer
 is added to a field in the locus_table (using the look up pointer) to point
 to the location of the allele_table entry, i.e.
 the locus_table in memory will point to the allele_table.
 (These 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

links
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are also very useful in R for performing merges of related tables.
 For example, in the above case, merge can produce a table with rows containing
 both locus and allele data.) Similar links are used to associate family
 data with person data, as well as phenotype and genotype data with a person.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Most tables are composed of integer, numeric and string data.
 The phenotype table is an exception.
 The normal trick of adding an extra column per row to indicate which phenotype'
s data is contained becomes more complicated because affection and quantitative
 data have different data types and number of values.
 Instead, the phenotype data for each person is one long byte vector composed
 of multiple values with 8 bytes of data per value either representing a
 quantitative value or an affection status value.
 Similarly, the genotype data are a raw vector with a pair of bytes representing
 a genotype for non bialleleic markers.
 For bialleleic markers, a raw compressed bit vector with two bits per genotype
 is used.
 (After the genotype data vectors are loaded into the database, you may
 select genotypes needed for your study.
 There is a function for a extracting a matrix of genotype data from the
 genotype table.).
 Note: the original compressed genotype vector created by Mega2 is one long
 vector.
 The genotype data written to the database is split into separate vectors
 for each chromosome.
 The end of each vector may have 0, 1, 2, or 3 unused bit pairs representing
 no markers.
 The Mega2 C++ program reassembles the vectors without these gaps.
 In addition, it can select specific chromosomes and/or specific markers
 needed for analysis and so does not need to read all the genotype bit vector
 data into active memory.
 The R version is not as sophisticated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rest of this document shows some examples of Mega2R package use.
 First, we discuss the main package, 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
r.
 Several additional packages, 
\emph on
mega2pedgene, mega2vcf and mega2genabel
\emph default
 are demonstrated which not only carry out the obvious functions but also
 illustrate how the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package and data frames can be used by other packages.
 After the Example section, the Implementation section discusses the highlights
 of each package.
 Note: The functions are discussed at an overview level.
 If you need programming details, look at the specific package documentation
 or the actual code.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is always difficult to share private study data.
 We have chosen to use the SeqSIMLA2 program to generate a dataset for us.
 [SeqSIMLA2: simulating correlated quantitative traits accounting for shared
 environmental effects in user-specified pedigree structure, Chung RH1,
 Tsai WY, Hsieh CH, Hung KY, Hsiung CA, Hauser ER., Genet Epidemiol.
 2015 Jan;39(1):20-4.
 doi: 10.1002/gepi.21850.
 Epub 2014 Sep 22.] We will construct a small PLINK ped dataset using the
 simulator, which is available from 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://sourceforge.net/projects/seqsimla/?source=typ_redirect
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 We started with the first example under the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

prevelance
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tab on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Simulate by Disease status
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tab on the tutorial page on the 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://seqsimla.nhri.org.tw/prevalence
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This simulation creates 1,380 samples of 500,000 markers; a bit too much
 for sharing.
 But a bit less than 200,000 markers are actually polymorphic while the
 rest are monomorphic; smaller but still too big.
 We chose to subsample the latter down to 1,380 people and 1,000 markers.
 These data will be used to illustrate Mega2 and Mega2R operations that
 follow.
\end_layout

