If you wish to use any of the Mega2R functions described here, you will
 initially have to run Mega2 to convert your data into an 'SQLite' database.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

Reading and Examining a Mega2 Database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

The 'mega2r' package facilitates reading genetic data from a Mega2-created
 'SQLite' database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph

Reading a Mega2 database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

After you have created the 'SQLite' database, start up the R program.
 Load the 'mega2r' package, then use the function `read.Mega2DB` to read
 a Mega2 database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

<<seqsimrdb,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

library(mega2r)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db", verbose = TRUE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

The first argument should be the path to the database.
 Providing the optional argument, `verbose`, causes the read function to
 summarize the tables created, their fields and their sizes.
 Finally, an "R environment", that contains the database tables is returned.
 (If you are unfamiliar with environments, you can think of them as data
 frames.
 `ENV$locus_table` will access the `locus_table` variable from `ENV` similar
 to fetching an "observation" from a data frame.
 The difference is when you change a data frame passed to a function, the
 change does not affect the original data frame.
 Only the function's local value is changed; ALL changes are forgotten when
 the function exits.
 If you change the data in an environment passed to a function, the change
 is permanent.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

<<seqsimrdb2>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db", verbose = TRUE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

We need to make two observations that apply to all Mega2R functions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph

Verbose Flag
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

When verbose is set in the initial read.Mega2DB, the value will be remembered.
 It may be used by any subsequent function.
 If verbose is TRUE, Mega2R functions can print diagnostic information.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph

Use of an Environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

All Mega2R functions that do not return an environment need to have an environme
nt supplied as an argument.
 As stated earlier, the environment is used to store the data frames that
 contain the 'SQLite' database.
 There are two ways to pass the environment.
 If you assigned the result of `read.Mega2DB` to the variable `seqsimr`,
 then you could supply the value seqsimr to any Mega2R function as the named
 argument `envir`:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

showMega2ENV(envir = seqsimr)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

The second choice is a bit of a "hack" but it is very convenient.
 Every Mega2R function (that does not return an environment) has a named
 `envir` argument defined to take on the default value `ENV`: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

envir = ENV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

as in
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

showMega2ENV = function(envir = ENV) { ...
 }
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

The code above, sets global variable `ENV` to the local variable, `envir`.
 Thus if `envir` is not provided in the function call, R will look up the
 value of `ENV` in the global environment.
 Hopefully, `ENV` was set to the return value of `read.Mega2DB` 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph

Back to more examples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

The `ls` function will show you all the variables in an environment.
 (You probably have used it without arguments to show you the variables
 in the .GlobalEnv.) Type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

<<ls>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

ls(ENV)
\end_layout

