\begin_layout Subsubsection
Iterating through Gene Transcripts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two ways to compute a function on all the genotypes (or markers)
 in a transcript.
 These examples are explained more thoroughly in Section 6 below.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
applyFnToRanges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R has an internal default list of the chromosome and base pair ranges
 for many gene transcripts.
 These transcripts come from the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly
 GRCH37.
 The list was further modified to eliminate multiple records from the same
 gene with the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 The list contains about 29,000 records.
 You may load your own range set instead of the default.
 You create a data frame that contains at least a name "observation", a
 chromosome "observation", a start position "observation" and an end position
 "observation".
 And you create an integer vector that contains the column numbers of the
 chromosome "observation", the start position "observation" and the end
 position "observation".
 These two become the arguments to the `setRanges` function, viz.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setRanges(Ranges, Columns)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, envir = ENV) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
goes through each transcript/range entry and finds the markers that fall
 within the bounds.
 It then builds a genotype matrix for the samples and each marker in the
 range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first argument of the `applyFnToRanges` function is a call back function.
 The function is called with four arguments: the genotype matrix, the markers
 in range, the selected transcript/range entry and the environment.
 The call back is invoked repeatedly for each transcript range that contains
 any markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If it is necessary to store information between successive invocations the
 environment (`envir`) can be used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
`applyFnToRanges` can also be provided explicit ranges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<applyF2R>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges, ENV$refIndices, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges[ENV$refRanges$chromosome ==1,],
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
       ENV$refIndices, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
applyFnToGenes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alternatively, you may search by genes.
 Here, the default transcript database is "TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene"
 from Bioconductor.
 Of course you can change it, by typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setAnnotations("txdb", "genedb")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where "txdb" is a string that is the name of transcript database that was
 fetched from Bioconductor, and similarly "genedb" is the name of a Bioconductor
 database that maps a gene id from the transcript to an entrez gene id.
 The `setAnnotations` command must be issued whenever R is started and the
 `mega2r` library has been loaded.
 By way of a reminder, Section 4.2 explains how to choose and install "TxDb.Hsapie
ns.UCSC.hg19.knownGene" or a different Annotation database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: We could require that you add to your session
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
library(<name of the transcription database>)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
but we have chosen not to.
 Instead, you just indicate the database name and the logic of `applyFnToGenes`
 does a `loadNamespace` of the string and then a `get` of the string from
 the namespace.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'KIF26B', 'AK5', 'ST7L'),
 envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
will find all the transcripts for each gene requested from the Bioconductor
 database.
 It will then behave like `applyFnToRanges`: find all the markers in each
 transcript range, compute the genotype matrix and then invoke the call
 back function with the required four arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The `applyFnToGenes` function has several other optional arguments that
 can specify complete chromosomes, (multiple) ranges of base pairs on chromosome
s, or collections of markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Iterating through the Genotypes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Encoded Genotypes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The heart of the above two functions is the calculation of the genotype
 matrix of samples by markers containing the nucleotides (strings).
 The genotype information is mostly stored in a compressed 2 bit representation.
 An Rcpp function does the conversion of the compressed genotype data to
 nucleotides.
 The function
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
getgenotypes(markers, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
returns the matrix for the specified markers.
 It can take one additional argument that supplies a string to separate
 the two alleles of each marker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Raw Genotypes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
getgenotypesraw(markers, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
is similar to the `getgenotypes` function except that the matrix it returns
 contains an integer encoding for each genotype.
 The integer's high 16 bits are the index for allele1 and the low 16 bits
 are the index for allele2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: There are actually two different Rcpp functions for each named function
 in Section 5.4.
 One function processes compressed genotype data and the other function
 processes uncompressed genotype data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Using Mega2R to carry out automated gene-based association tests using 'pedgene'
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R provides functions that permit one to run the 'pedgene' package to
 carry out gene-based association tests on family data looping over selected
 marker subsets.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'pedgene' package implements methods for carrying out gene-based association
 tests on family data, and is available on CRAN as (https://CRAN.R-project.org/pac
kage=pedgene).
 It was written by Daniel Schaid and Jason Sinnwell: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[Schaid DJ, McDonnell SK., Sinnwell JP, Thibodeau SN.
 (2013) Multiple Genetic Variant Association Testing by Collapsing and Kernel
 Methods With Pedigree or Population Structured Data, Genet Epidemiol, 37(5):409
-18.]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Gene transcripts reminder
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you plan to select transcripts by gene name, you must load them from
 Bioconductor.
 In Section 4.2, we indicated that you needed to type once to install the
 package:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<bioc2>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
source("https://bioconductor.org/biocLite.R")
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
biocLite("TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene") 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
biocLite("org.Hs.eg.db")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And then, to use the desired transcription data base, use this command from
 the 'mega2r' package as part of your session:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setAnnotations(txdb, entrezGene)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where `txdb` is the name of Bioconductor transcription database, and `entrezGene
` is the name of Bioconductor mapping of gene name or gene alias to entrez
 gene id.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Loading a Mega2 database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rather than read the Mega2 'SQLite' database with the `read.Mega2DB` function
 described previously, here we use a specialized `init_pedgene` function
 to read the Mega2 database.
 This latter function calls a utility function also used by `read.Mega2DB`.
 Then it creates, edits, and rewrites the family data, storing it in a `pedgene`
-compatible data frame, `fam` .
 (`fam` merges data from the `pedigree_table`, `person_table` and `phenotype_tab
le`.) `init_pedgene` purges persons with unknown case/control status which
 is necessary for the pedgene calculation.
 (When `fam` is filtered, similar adjustments are made to the phenotype_table
 and the genotype_table.) Finally, `init_pedgene` calculates some values
 that will be used repeatedly and stores them in the environment that is
 returned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<init_pedgene,message=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
ENV = init_pedgene("seqsimr.db")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Running pedgene on transcripts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R has an internal default list of the chromosome and base pair ranges
 for a number of gene transcripts.
 These transcripts come from the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly
 GRCH37.
 The list was further modified to eliminate multiple records of the same
 gene with the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 These data contain about 29,000 records.
 You may load your own range set instead of the default.
 You create a data frame that contains at least a name "observation", a
 chromosome "observation", a start position "observation" and an end position
 "observation".
 Then, you create an integer vector that contains the column numbers of
 the chromosome "observation", the start position "observation" and the
 end position "observation".
 These two objects are then arguments to the `setRanges` function, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setRanges(Transcripts, Columns)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By default, the function `run_pedgene` examines the first 100 transcripts
 and prints the results.
 For this database, the first 100 transcripts identifies only one transcript
 with several markers (It is found because it is on chromosome 1 and it
 has a range that overlaps with some of the markers in our data.) To make
 this tutorial exercise run faster, we noticed that the identified transcript
 appeared at transcript 54; so we will restrict pedgene to a small range
 of transcripts around 54, viz.
 50 through 60.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: `verbose` needs to be TRUE, for these diagnostics to be printed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<run_pedgene>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
ENV$verbose=TRUE
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
run_pedgene(gs=50:60)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You will see many reports of "No markers in range", because the database
 only contains markers on a subrange of chromosome 1 whereas the transcripts
 span the entire genome.
 Occasionally you will see a listing of a gene name, markers, and count
 of 1/1, 1/2 and 2/2 genotypes, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24037 520 646 214 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24039 940 386 54 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24041 1377 3 0 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24048 860 460 60 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24494 789 501 90 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24499 891 442 47 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24506 891 442 47 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24507 789 501 90
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The genotype matrix for these markers, along with the markers, the range
 used, and the environment are passed to the call back function `DOpedgene`.
 `DOpedgene` converts the genotype nucleotides 1/1, 1/2 and 2/2 to the values
 0, 1 and 2 (or 2, 1, 0) if 1/1 is genotype homozygous for the allele with
 the minor allele frequency.
 Then it runs pedgene.
 The results are automatically stored in a data frame with "observations":
 prefix of chromosome, gene, number of markers and base pair range followed
 by Pedgene data: kernel and burden, value and p-values, four values for
 each of three weightings of the markers.
 These data are saved in the data frame, `pedgene_results`, in the environment.
 They are also printed when `verbose` is TRUE, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The results are always appended to the data frame.
 You should truncate it when necessary.
\end_layout

