\begin_layout Subsubsection
Running pedgene on selected genes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You may try searching for transcripts of specific genes.
 Here, the default transcript database is `TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene`
 from Bioconductor.
 Of course you can change it.
 Type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setAnnotations("txdb", "genedb")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where "txdb" is a string that is the name of transcript database that was
 fetched from Bioconductor, and similarly "genedb" is the name of a Bioconductor
 database that maps a gene id from the input to an entrez gene id.
 You need to install any new database with biocLite, as shown earlier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We leave the command below as an exercise.
 It runs a bit slowly.
 It needs to find all the transcripts for each gene; then it needs to find
 all the markers between each pair of transcript start/end ranges; and finally
 it needs to do a small computation on the genotypes from each set of markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<applyF2G,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'KIF26B', 'AK5', 'ST7L'),
 envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But let us run this function for a few genes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<applyFnToGenes,eval=TRUE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'AK5'), envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The default behavior of `DOpedgene` is to append any new results to
 the end of the
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
`ENV$pedgene_results` data frame.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: You will observe that for several genes there are a few transcripts
 with the same or overlapping start values and end values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Outputting Mega2R data to VCF format
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The `Mega2VCF` function permits one to output subsets of the 'mega2r' data
 as a VCF file, accompanied by related files (for phenotype and family data).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The VCF data format (http://www.internationalgenome.org/wiki/Analysis/Variant%20Ca
ll%20Format/vcf-variant-call-format-version-40/) was originally defined
 by the 1000 Genomes Project (http://www.internationalgenome.org/home) for
 data storage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The current version of data format can be found at (http://samtools.github.io/hts-
specs/).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Writing a VCF File using Mega2R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, create the directory "vcfr", so we can keep all the generated data
 in one place.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<mega2vcflib>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if (! dir.exists("vcfr")) dir.create("vcfr")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2VCF function takes an argument which is the prefix used on all
 generated files.
 We will make that a path that includes the directory 
\series bold
vcfr
\series default
.
 The Mega2VCF function assumes the Mega2 data frames are contained in the
 global variable ENV.
 If this is not the case, supply a named argument `envir` set to the environment
 you wish to use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that Mega2VCF will create the .vcf file as well as a .fam file (in linkage
 format), a .phe file (phenotypes) and a few others.
 These files are listed below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Creating the VCF file and related files is accomplished by typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<mega2vcf>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Mega2VCF("vcfr/vcf.01", ENV$markers[ENV$markers$chromosome==1 ,])
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The line above places the data for only chromosome 1 in the files
 (Recall that our simulated data is only on chromosome 1).
 Note: In general, you can filter `ENV$markers` as needed.
 If the second argument is not present, all the markers are written out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The generated files are listed below:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<lsvcf>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
list.files("vcfr")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can read the code to see how these files are created from the data frames.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Converting Mega2R data to GenABEL format
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R provides support for converting the data frames into 'GenABEL' format
 (e.g.
 as gwaa.data-class objects).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'GenABEL' package is available on CRAN as (https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=
GenABEL).
 The reference is [Aulchenko Y.S., Ripke S., Isaacs A., van Duijn C.M.
 GenABEL: an R package for genome-wide association analysis.
 Bioinformatics.
 2007 23(10):1294-6.]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Creating a GenABEL gwaa.data-class object using Mega2R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several data formats that Mega2 can read and transform into a
 database that are not directly accepted by GenABEL.
 But any Mega2 database that can be read into R, can be transformed to GenABEL
 following this example using 
\series bold
seqsimr.db
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GenABEL (http://www.genabel.org/) can process PLINK .tped/.tfam files with the
 function `convert.snp.tped` to create a GenABEL "raw" file.
 Then the "raw" file and a generated phenotype file can be processed by
 the function, `load.gwaa.data`, to yield a gwaa.data-class object.
 Currently, the function, `Mega2GenABEL`, uses this mechanism to convert
 Mega2 data frames to a gwaa.data-class object.
 The argument to `Mega2GenABEL` is a file name prefix that will be used
 to name the .tped/.fam/.phe files that are created.
 The GenABEL functions `convert.snp.tped` and `load.gwaa.data` are explained
 in https://cran.r-project.org/package=GenABEL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you haven't already, first install the 'GenABEL' package from CRAN.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<seqsimgwaa>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db")
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
# This line converts the database to a gwaa.data-class object.
 The intermediate
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
# files begin with "SEQ"
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
seqsimgwaa = Mega2GenABEL("SEQ")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The files generated are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<lsseq>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
list.files(pattern = "SEQ.*")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can use any of the GenABEL provided functions on the results, `seqsimgwaa`.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
<<strseqsimgwaa>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
str(seqsimgwaa)
\end_layout

